A||||7||Uaana||Door prob and dogbone||||5||
Z||000000||Uaana||01-07-2000||04:32 AM||||Couple of quick questions.<BR>1. The door on my 87 GT rattles over bumps.<BR>Is this bad bushings, or bad nader pin align?<BR>2. I need to replace my dog bone (bad rubber)<BR>is it worth the extra expense for the poly replacement or should I stick to oem?<P>My thanks in advance.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||01-07-2000||10:26 AM||||"Rattle" is a pretty generic term...does something rattle inside the door or can you see movement in the entire door when going over bumps ? Also, when you look at the side of your car, does the door look aligned with the side of the car (does the trim "match up")...if the door looks out of alignment, probably need new bushings and pin (try the "search" function for info on a fix).<P>As to the dogbone, go with the poly...transmits a little more vibration than stock, but better for the car. If you have a bone without the ridge inside, you can buy the replacement poly bushings for $17 from <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.suspension.com/fiero</A> <P>They basically slip in once the old rubber is removed.||||reg||10
Z||000002||wwh0001||01-07-2000||10:41 AM||||The door pins and bushings do wear and can cause the doors to "rattle" raplacing them can fix this or for a quick temporary fix wrap tape or velcro strips around the door sriker. This makes the door close tighter and somtimes stops the rattle; at least for awhile.<BR>as for the poly bushings for the dogbone it depends on what you want. if you don't want vibration, don't get it. if you don't mind the vibration the poly will last longer and your engine moves less. personally I don't like the poly, but most on the forum would not agree.||||reg||1
Z||000003||wwh0001||01-07-2000||10:41 AM||||Double Post<P>[This message has been edited by wwh0001 (edited 01-07-2000).]<P>[This message has been edited by wwh0001 (edited 01-07-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by wwh0001 (edited 01-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||ka4nkf||01-07-2000||05:34 PM||||The dogbone with poly does vibrate more but i noticed that it gets better after a few miles. I have about 300 miles on mine and it does not vibrate much at all. I would say go with the poly dogbone||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||01-07-2000||06:24 PM||||I noticed Suspension.com has raised the price of the replacement bushings from $17 to $20.<P>If you go to poly, you will either have to get an old dogbone without the ridges inside so you can use the replacement bushings or buy a new bone. At least that's what I understand on an '87 GT. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||01-07-2000||07:44 PM||||the pin/bushing kits are in the red "help" blister packs at any autoparts store. you need 2 sets for each door. It'll run you about 9$us per door.<P>When you replace the bottom hinge parts slip a greased nylon washer in the top between the door half and the post half. One like the oil drain plug uses works best. This improves the clearance in the hinge, and spreads out the load of the door. It makes the brass bushings less likely to go south again soon. (Normally the top of the brass wears off in no time. then the hinge sections drag.) There isn't enough area to do this for the top hinge but it reall doesn't need it. [[Note: you may have to adjust the bottom hinge a bit after replaceing the pins. The bolt heads are inside the door.]] <P>the door latch and strike aren't adjustable in allot of Fiero. The strikes are suposed to be but they aren't. <P>The hinges have no adjustment on the post. You can adjust the door in and out some.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||01-07-2000||08:41 PM||||ogre--You are a brilliant man, but there's only one bolt head inside the car.  The rest (top and bottom) are outside (and a real *****  to get to).||||reg||1
